{"author":{"id":"f4e4dbe251fbd7519eab696986bae317e93a786739d6cebe9c4c1e89dd44e465"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793513529,"nonce":"Iijh9iqhkGrP6oeg8gGf41T1yhY=","title":"Epic: LeanSharp roadmap (milestones M0–M8)","message":"LeanSharp aims to give machine-checked proofs about ordinary C# code: selected methods are translated into the Calculus of Inductive Constructions and checked by a C# port of the Lean 4 kernel, with Lean's libraries available as imported declarations (`README.md`, `docs/design.md`). The work is staged as milestones M0–M8 in `docs/design.md` §11. Until now that plan lived only in the design doc and commit history; this epic tracks it in git-bug so each stage has a ticket with its exit criterion and evidence.\n\n\nA developer marks a C# method `[Verified]`, writes a `[Theorem]` about it, and the build fails with the unsolved goal shown when the theorem is false. Contracts on effectful code are checked with external dependencies treated as reported hypotheses (D9), and proof certificates can be exported for re-checking by Lean's own kernel (D6).\n\n\n- Direction B (D1): verify existing C#; never generate C# from verified terms.\n- The kernel stays the only trusted proof checker; tactics, VC generation, server and cache are untrusted (design §9).\n- Each milestone's exit criterion is the one in design §11. Changes to a criterion are recorded on the milestone's ticket.\n\n\nAll milestone tickets below are closed with their exit criteria verified, or their remaining scope is explicitly deferred on the ticket.\n\n\nListed in a follow-up comment once the IDs exist.\n\nMilestones are sequential: each one depends on the previous one being complete.","files":null}]}